WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.840
Your eyes work in starlight and in bright sun.

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.460
How can one kind of cell cope with both?

3
00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:13.883
Between starlight and sunlight, the light changes a hundred-million-fold or more.

4
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:20.307
Yet a dark-adapted rod goes from barely responding to saturated over about a

5
00:00:20.307 --> 00:00:21.767
hundredfold range of flashes.

6
00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:26.997
In darkness, cGMP holds channels open in the outer segment.

7
00:00:27.837 --> 00:00:29.397
A steady dark current flows in.

8
00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:35.237
Light activates PDE, which breaks down cGMP, and channels close.

9
00:00:36.237 --> 00:00:38.377
Each response switches off part of that current.

10
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.920
So light spends the current.

11
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.920
What is still flowing limits how big the next response can be.

12
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.430
Two terms. Gain is how much current one absorbed photon switches off, and for how

13
00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:52.430
long.

14
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:57.653
Sensitivity is the response to a dim flash, divided by the strength of that flash.

15
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:02.117
Predict. Suppose a rod's gain never changed.

16
00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:05.017
What would a modest steady background do to it?

17
00:01:10.917 --> 00:01:12.857
It would close nearly every channel.

18
00:01:13.757 --> 00:01:17.477
The rod would be saturated, unable to signal any further brightening.

19
00:01:18.297 --> 00:01:20.357
Only dimming the light would let current return.

20
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.510
So a photoreceptor turns its own gain down as the background brightens.

21
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:27.993
Calcium feedback helps.

22
00:01:28.853 --> 00:01:32.833
While the light stays on, it reopens some channels, keeping current in reserve.

23
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:36.293
It also cuts and shortens each new response.

24
00:01:37.253 --> 00:01:38.333
Each photon does less.

25
00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.467
The result is called Weber behaviour.

26
00:01:42.487 --> 00:01:46.667
On brighter backgrounds, sensitivity falls roughly as one over the background.

27
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.400
Contrast is a change in light, as a fraction of the background.

28
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:58.150
The payoff. On bright backgrounds, take small, brief flashes of the same contrast

29
00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.490
and the same duration.

30
00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:04.910
Their photon count grows with the background, while sensitivity falls just as fast.

31
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.770
So their peak responses stay about the same.

32
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:10.133
Now remove the GCAPs.

33
00:02:11.073 --> 00:02:14.293
In darkness, a rod's response to one photon gets larger.

34
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:17.957
But on a background it keeps less current in reserve.

35
00:02:18.937 --> 00:02:20.797
Its sensitivity falls more steeply.

36
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.540
This picture comes mainly from mouse rods and cones.

37
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.500
A bright enough light can still saturate them.

38
00:02:28.433 --> 00:02:33.753
Pigment bleaching, light using up visual pigment, and other mechanisms extend the

39
00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:34.813
range in bright light.

40
00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:38.203
One feedback loop does most of this work.

41
00:02:38.943 --> 00:02:40.203
The test is to cut it.

42
00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:46.367
The takeaway. Light spends a limited current, and calcium feedback keeps some in

43
00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:46.687
reserve.
